stem_md:
Is it possible to arrange  $n \times  n$  in the cells of a square table the numbers  $0$ , $ 1$  or  $2$  so that the sums of the numbers in rows and columns took on all different values from  $1$  to  $2n$ ? Consider two cases: 
a)  $n$  is an odd number;
b)  $n$  is an even number.

solution_md:
If we solve the quadratic equation for  $sin(x)$ , we obtain: $sin(x) = \frac{-p \pm \sqrt{p^2-4q}}{2}$  (i)

of which  $q\le \frac{p^2}{4}$  is a necessary condition for  $sin(x)$  to be real-valued.  Since  $-1 \le sin(x)\le1$  for all  $x \in \mathbb{R}$ , we next obtain: $-1 \le \frac{-p \pm \sqrt{p^2-4q}}{2} \le 1$ ;

or  $\frac{-p+\sqrt{p^2-4q}}{2} \le 1$  AND  $-1 \le \frac{-p-\sqrt{p^2-4q}}{2}$ ;

or  $q \ge -p-1$  AND  $q \ge p-1$ .

Thus, the region  $\Omega$  of the  $pq-$ plane where a solution exists for  $sin^{2}(x) + p \cdot sin(x) + q=0$  consists of: $\textcolor{red}{\Omega\{(p,q): \{p \in [-2,0); q \in [-p-1, p^2/4]\} \cup \{p \in [0,2]; q \in [p-1,p^2/4]\}\}}$ .

For the positive solutions associated with the original quadratic, we will require: $0<\frac{-p+\sqrt{p^2-4q}}{2} \le 1$  AND  $-1 \le \frac{-p-\sqrt{p^2-4q}}{2}<0$ ;

or  $0 > q \ge -p-1$  AND  $0 > q \ge p-1$ .

Multiplying the above equation through by  $cos(x)$ , we obtain: $6\sqrt{2}sin^{2}(x) - 2\sqrt{2}sin(x)+[3sin(x)-1]cos(x)=0$ ;

or  $6\sqrt{2}sin^{2}(x) - 2\sqrt{2}sin(x)+[3sin(x)-1]\sqrt{1-sin^{2}(x)}=0$ ;

or  $72sin^{4}(x) -48sin^{3}(x) +8sin^{2}(x) = [1-6sin(x)+9sin^{2}(x)][1-sin^{2}(x)]$ ;

or  $72sin^{4}(x) -48sin^{3}(x) +8sin^{2}(x) = 1-6sin(x)+8sin^{2}(x) +6sin^{3}(x)-9sin^{4}(x)$ ;

or  $81sin^{4}(x) -54sin^{3}(x) +6sin(x)-1=0$ ;

or  $[3sin(x)-1]^{3}[3sin(x)+1]=0$ ;

or  $sin(x) = \pm \frac{1}{3}$ .

Since  $0 \le sin(x) \le 1$  over  $x \in [0^{\circ}, 180^{\circ}] \Rightarrow$  there exists two such solutions over this same interval:  $\textcolor{red}{x = arcsin\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)=19.47^{\circ}, 160.53^{\circ}}$ .

