 $\Rightarrow x+\sqrt{x^2+1}=\frac{1}{y+\sqrt{y^2+1}}
=\sqrt{y^2+1}-y
\Rightarrow x^2+y^2+2xy=x^2+y^2+2-2\sqrt{x^2y^2+x^2+y^2+1}
\Rightarrow x^2y^2-2xy+1=x^2y^2+x^2+y^2+1
\Rightarrow (x+y)^2=0\Rightarrow x+y=0$

\begin{align*}
\dfrac1{x^2+2xy+y^2}+\dfrac1{x^2}+\dfrac1{y^2}&\ge\dfrac9{4xy} 
1+\dfrac{x^2+2xy+y^2}{x^2}+\dfrac{x^2+2xy+y^2}{y^2}&\ge\dfrac94\left(\dfrac{x^2+2xy+y^2}{xy}\right) 
1+1+\dfrac{2y}x+\dfrac{y^2}{x^2}+1+\dfrac{2x}y+\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}&\ge\dfrac94\left(\dfrac xy+2+\dfrac yx\right) 
3+\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}+\dfrac{y^2}{x^2}-\dfrac x{4y}-\dfrac y{4x}&\ge\dfrac92 
\left(\dfrac xy\right)^2+2\left(\dfrac xy\right)\left(\dfrac yx\right)+\left(\dfrac yx\right)^2&\ge\dfrac72+\dfrac14\left(\dfrac xy+\dfrac yx\right) 
\left(\dfrac xy+\dfrac yx\right)^2&\ge\dfrac72+\dfrac14\left(\dfrac xy+\dfrac yx\right) 
a&=\dfrac xy+\dfrac yx 
a^2&\ge\dfrac72+\dfrac a4 
a^2-2a\left(\dfrac18\right)+\dfrac1{64}&\ge\dfrac72+\dfrac1{64} 
\left(a-\dfrac18\right)^2&\ge\dfrac{225}{64} 
a-\dfrac18&\ge\dfrac{15}8 
a&\ge\dfrac{16}8 
a&\ge2. 
\end{align*}

It is a well-known fact that the sum of a number and its reciprocal is always at least  $2$ . Thus, it is clear that  $a=\dfrac xy+\dfrac yx\ge2$ . This fact that is derived from the original equation is true, and we can easily reverse our steps for a full proof. QED.

