\end{align*}
However, we are done after we note that if  $D$  is the foot from  $A$  to  $BC$ ,  $BA\cos B=D$  and  $CA\cos C=CD$ , so the two values sum are equal, as desired.

solution_md:
Stewart's yields  $PA^2+PB\cdot PC=\frac{AB^2\cdot PC+AC^2\cdot PB}{BC}$ . Using Cauchy-Schwarz, \begin{align*}
\left(\frac{AB^2}{PC}+\frac{AC^2}{PB} \right)\left( AB^2\cdot PC+AC^2\cdot PB\right)\geq (AB^2+AC^2)^2=BC^4,
\end{align*}which rearranges to  $\frac{AB^2}{PC}+\frac{AC^2}{PB} \geq \frac{BC^3}{\frac{AB^2\cdot PC+AC^2\cdot PB}{BC}}=\frac{BC^3}{PA^2 + PB \cdot PC}$ , as desired.

Let  $r$  be a primitive root modulo  $p$ . It's quite well-known that  $\{1,2,3,\ldots,p-1\}$  are congruent to  $\{r,r^2,\ldots,r^{p-1}\}$  modulo  $p$  in some order. So the given sum is nothing but 
\begin{align*}
1^n+2^n+3^n+\ldots+(p-1)^n &\equiv \underbrace{r^n+r^{2n}+r^{3n}+\ldots+r^{(p-1)n}}_{\text{In some order}} \pmod{p}
&\equiv r^n \left(\frac{r^{(p-1)n}-1}{r^n-1}\right) \pmod{p} 
&\equiv 0 \pmod{p} 
\end{align*}
which shows what we desired  $\square$

